C64/UX 0.2.0

New shell, RAM filesystem, keyboard extensions, disk interface, game loader,
ROM RUNNER game, build scripts and tests: Apache License, Version 2.0.
See LICENSE.

The starting Open Kernal 64 source and standalone 6502 assembler were supplied
by the user in kernal-private-eddd-current-v2.zip and carry
SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0. Their original SPDX markings are retained.
The new paired images replace the supplied BASIC interpreter and remove its
floating-point support from the KERNAL.

The supplied open-character-rom64.bin and open-1541-dos.bin are copied unchanged
from that user-provided archive. Corresponding emulator-named files are exact
copies or 8 KiB splits. Their sources and a separate license document were not
included in the supplied archive. No new license grant for those binary assets
is asserted here. See docs/PROVENANCE.json for source hashes.

The optional profiles/classic/roms directory preserves the user-supplied
Open BASIC and Open KERNAL binaries unchanged, alongside the same character
and drive assets. It does not contain Commodore's proprietary original ROMs.
The complete source needed to rebuild that supplied BASIC binary was not in
the input archive. This package makes no additional license grant for those
inherited binaries and does not claim to reproduce the classic profile from
source. Its manifest records each exact binary.

SERPENT 64 v1.0.4, Copyright (c) 2026 AdvancingDevelopment, is redistributed
unchanged under the MIT license. Its original LICENSE.txt and README.txt are
included in third_party/serpent64. The project's Apache-2.0 license does not
replace that game's license. Release URL, commit and hashes are recorded in
third_party/serpent64/PROVENANCE.json.

The independent test runner uses py65 (BSD license), installed separately.
The optional integration harness builds against the user's C64Emulator source;
that repository and its dependencies are not redistributed in this package.

C64/UX is an independent Unix-like firmware experiment. It contains no Linux
kernel code and does not implement the Linux ABI. Product names identify
compatibility targets, not affiliation or endorsement.
